**Calling `/predict` without `location` — why does it still work?**
`location` is declared as an *optional* query parameter with a default
value (`location: str = "other"`). FastAPI only requires a query parameter
when it has no default, so when `location` is omitted the endpoint simply
falls back to `"other"` instead of raising an error.

**Calling `/predict` without `area` — why does it return a 422?**
`area` has no default value, so FastAPI treats it as a *required* query
parameter. When a required parameter is missing, FastAPI's automatic
request validation (built on Pydantic) rejects the request before the
endpoint function even runs, returning a `422 Unprocessable Entity` with a
JSON body explaining which field was missing.

**Why does a relative URL (`/predict?...`) work in `fetch()` now?**
Because Task 4 mounts the frontend page itself on the FastAPI app
(`http://127.0.0.1:8000/static/house_form.html`), the HTML page and the
`/predict` API now share the exact same origin (protocol + host + port).
A relative URL is resolved by the browser against the page's own origin,
so `/predict` automatically becomes
`http://127.0.0.1:8000/predict`. Since there's no cross-origin request
involved, the browser doesn't need CORS headers to allow it — this is the
same-origin trick the assignment uses instead of configuring CORS
middleware.
